## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128 bit manipulations and zero edge cases without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Branchless bit tricks
- 128 bit layout as hi and lo
- De Bruijn based ctz for 64 and 32 bit values
- Handling zero input gracefully

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline int ctz64(uint64_t x) {
  if (x == 0) return 64;
  static const int tab[64] = { /* 64 entries */ };
  uint64_t y = x & -x;
  return tab[(y * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ULL) >> 58];
}

int lsbIndex128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  int a = ctz64(lo);
  int b = 64 + ctz64(hi);
  int m = - (int)(lo != 0);
  int res = (a & m) | (b & ~m);
  return (lo | hi) == 0 ? -1 : res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if 128 bit intrinsics are unavailable
- Extend to msbIndex128 with branchless patterns

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to perform a branchless, cross-boundary bitwise scan on a 128-bit value—a common primitive in cryptography and systems programming. It verifies you can correctly propagate carries and parity across the 64-bit lane boundary without branching.

## Key Concepts

- **Parallel prefix (scan) technique**: Each shift-XOR step doubles the bit-range being folded, achieving O(log n) instead of O(n) operations.
- **128-bit right shift implementation**: Requires explicit carry propagation between the hi and lo 64-bit words.
- **Branchless design**: No conditionals ensures constant-time execution, critical for crypto applications.

## Code Example

```javascript
function prefixXor128(x_lo, x_hi) {
  let lo = x_lo, hi = x_hi;
  // 128-bit shift helper: (lo, hi) >> k
  const shift128 = (lo, hi, k) => {
    if (k < 64) return { lo: (lo >>> k) | ((hi & ((1n << k) - 1n)) << (64 - k)), hi: hi >>> k };
    return { lo: hi >>> (k - 64), hi: 0 };
  };
  // Parallel prefix: x ^= x >> {1,2,4,8,16,32,64}
  for (const k of [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64]) {
    const s = shift128(lo, hi, k);
    lo ^= s.lo; hi ^= s.hi;
  }
  return { lo, hi };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for a prefix OR or prefix AND?
- What changes are needed to handle SIMD/vector implementations?
- How does this compare to a naive loop-based approach in terms of cycle count?

Why this is asked
- Tests ability to implement 128-bit rotation without branching, using a safe 128-bit intermediate. Covers edge cases n=0 and n=64 cleanly.

Key concepts
- 128-bit arithmetic, circular shift, branch-free implementation, handling wrap-around.

Code example
```c
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
  __uint128_t full = ((__uint128_t)x.hi << 64) | x.lo;
  unsigned k = n & 127;
  __uint128_t r = (full << k) | (full >> (128 - k));
  uint128 out; out.hi = (uint64_t)(r >> 64); out.lo = (uint64_t)r; return out;
}
```

Follow-up questions
- How would you implement rotateRight128 without 128-bit type?
- Discuss performance when 128-bit shifts are not optimized by the compiler.

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a branchless, constant-time population-count across 128 bits using only shifts and masks, a common low-level optimization in crypto/signature code.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register)
- 64-bit popcount, extended to 128-bit
- Branchless arithmetic for performance

## Code Example

```c
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t w) {
  w = w - ((w >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  w = (w & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((w >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  w = (w + (w >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (unsigned int)((w * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56);
}
unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
  unsigned int hi = popcount64(x.hi);
  unsigned int lo = popcount64(x.lo);
  return hi + lo;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for variable-width counts (e.g., 256-bit)?
- What are the trade-offs compared to using hardware popcnt when available?

## Why This Is Asked

Parity computation over 128 bits tests the ability to design constant-time reductions using only bitwise operations and shifts, without loops or branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise XOR folding across 128 bits
- Progressive reduction by shifting (64,32,16,8,4,2,1)
- Final extraction with a single bit AND

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128_t;
unsigned parity128(uint128_t x) {
  uint64_t v = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  v ^= v >> 32;
  v ^= v >> 16;
  v ^= v >> 8;
  v ^= v >> 4;
  v ^= v >> 2;
  v ^= v >> 1;
  return v & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to 256 bits while preserving the same approach?
- Can you prove the operation is constant time regardless of input bit distribution?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of per-byte bit-twiddling, branchless design, and parallel bit tricks that map well to performance-critical code paths in systems programming.

## Key Concepts
- Byte-wide parity, parallel bit tricks, constant-time operations
- Masking to extract per-byte indicators
- No branches or loops

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t perByteParity64(uint64_t x) {
  uint64_t y = x;
  y ^= y >> 1;
  y ^= y >> 2;
  y ^= y >> 4;
  return y & 0x0101010101010101ULL;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 32-bit blocks? 
- Can you adapt to compute even parity per byte instead of odd parity?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of 128-bit data manipulation, endianness, and composing simple bit tricks to achieve a larger transformation without loops.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit value composed of two 64-bit halves
- Byte reversal within 64-bit words (endian swap)
- Combining results to form final 128-bit reversal
- No loops; relies on fixed-mask shifts

## Code Example

```javascript
uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x = ((x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32) | ((x & 0xFFFFFFFF00000000ULL) >> 32);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x & 0xFFFF0000FFFF0000ULL) >> 16);
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8)  | ((x & 0xFF00FF00FF00FF00ULL) >> 8);
  return x;
}

void endianSwap128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo){
  *out_hi = reverse64(lo);
  *out_lo = reverse64(hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify correctness for non-trivial patterns quickly?
- How does this approach scale if you extend to 256-bit values?

